884":"Beersheba","H6213":"do","H3477":"upright","H5869":"eye","H3068":"LORD","H3605":"all","H3117":"day","H3077":"Jehoiada","H3548":"priest","H5375":"raise","H802":"woman","H8147":"two","H3205":"to beget","H1323":"daughter","H1961":"to be","H310":"after","H3651":"right","H5973":"with","H3820":"heart","H2318":"to renew","H853":"[Obj.]","H1004":"place","H6908":"to gather","H3881":"Levi","H559":"to say","H3318":"come","H5892":"excitement","H3063":"Judah","H3478":"Israel","H3701":"money","H2388":"strengthen","H430":"God","H1767":"sufficiency","H859":"you(m.s.)","H4116":"to hasten","H1697":"word","H3808":"not","H7121":"call to","H4428":"king","H7218":"head","H4069":"why?","H1875":"to seek","H5921":"upon","H935":"come","H4864":"tribute","H4872":"Moses","H5650":"servant/slave","H6951":"assembly","H168":"tent","H5715":"testimony","H3588":"for","H6271":"Athaliah","H4849":"wickedness","H6555":"to break through","H1571":"also","H6944":"holiness","H1168":"Baal","H727":"ark","H259":"one","H5414":"give","H8179":"gate","H2351":"outside","H6963":"voice","H4057":"mouth","H8055":"to rejoice","H8269":"ruler","H5971":"people","H7993":"to throw","H5704":"till","H3615":"finish","H6256":"time","H413":"to(wards)","H6486":"punishment","H3027":"hand","H7200":"see","H7227":"many","H5608":"to recount","H6496":"overseer","H6168":"to uncover","H7725":"return","H4725":"place","H3541":"thus","H622":"to gather","H7230":"abundance","H4399":"work","H5656":"service","H7936":"to hire","H2672":"to hew","H2796":"artificer","H1270":"iron","H5178":"bronze","H5927":"rise","H724":"health","H5975":"stand","H4971":"tally","H553":"to strengthen","H6440":"before","H7605":"remnant","H3627":"article/utensil","H8335":"ministry","H3709":"palm","H2091":"gold","H5930":"burnt offering","H8548":"continually","H2204":"be old","H7646":"to satisfy","H4191":"to die","H3967":"hundred","H7970":"thirty","H4194":"death","H6912":"to bury","H1732":"David","H2896":"pleasant","H7812":"to bow","H227":"then","H8085":"hear","H5800":"forsake","H1":"father","H5647":"to serve","H842":"Asherah","H6091":"idol","H7110":"wrath","H819":"guiltiness","H2063":"this","H7971":"depart","H5030":"prophet","H5749":"to uphold","H238":"to listen","H7307":"spirit","H3847":"to clothe","H2148":"Zechariah","H4100":"what?","H5674":"to pass","H4687":"commandment","H6743":"to rush","H7194":"to conspire","H7275":"to stone","H68":"stone","H2691":"court","H2142":"to remember","H2617":"kindness","H834":"which","H2026":"to kill","H8622":"circuit","H2426":"rampart","H758":"Aram","H7843":"to ruin","H7998":"spoil","H1834":"Damascus","H4705":"little","H376":"man","H2428":"Helech","H3966":"much","H854":"with","H8201":"judgment","H3212":"to walk","H4480":"from","H4251":"suffering","H1818":"blood","H4296":"bed","H6913":"grave","H428":"these","H2066":"Zabad","H8100":"Shimeath","H5985":"Ammonitess","H3075":"Jehozabad","H8116":"Shimrith","H4125":"Moabite","H7235":"to multiply","H4853":"burden","H3247":"(Gate of the) Foundation","H2005":"look!","H3789":"to write","H4097":"story","H5612":"document","H558":"Amaziah","H8478":"under"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Him. Joas, as Protestants (Haydock) and most interpreters understand it, with the Syriac, &c. Joiada was too old to think of marrying again, as the Rabbins would explain the Hebrew, conformably to the Septuagint and Arabic, “himself.” (Calmet) — Joas did not probably marry two at once. (Salien, the year of the world 3766.) — Amasias was born of Joadan, chap, xxv. 1. (Menochius) — The high priest considered Joas as his son, being married to his aunt. (Haydock)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Money. The half sicle, (Exodus xxx. 13., and 4 Kings xii. 4.; Calmet) which at first was required for the tabernacle, and afterwards for the repairs of the temple. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Chief “priest,” (ver. 11,) where he is called the first. Hebrew, “the head priest.”"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Children. All but Ochozias had been slain by the Arabs. The plural is often put for the singular. St. Jerome (Trad.) explains it of the priests of the idols, whom Athalia cherished as her own offspring."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","That, &c. Hebrew, “till it was done,” according to the law. (Pagnin)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","From day. Literally, “every day,” (Haydock) when the chest was replenished. (Menochius)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","And for. Hebrew, “and to offer,” such as (Haydock) “mortars,” Proverbs xxvii. 22. Eali is thus translated. (Calmet) — Protestants, “and spoons and vessels.”"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Israel, particularly to those of the kingdom of Juda. (Haydock) — His, David’s, house, (Menochius) as he had restored the crown to his offspring."],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Worshipped. Hebrew, “bowed down to the king, and he hearkened to them.” (Haydock) — The Jews say that the princes flattered him with the title of god, as he had been educated in the temple. (Estius) — But this is improbable. (Calmet) — They petitioned for leave to re-establish the worship of Baal, (Menochius) and paid greater honours to the king than were becoming. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Idols. Hebrew, “sorrows.” Septuagint, “the Astartes and idols.” (Calmet)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Them. Calling heaven and earth to witness (Calmet) the unity of God. (Haydock) — These prophets might have been performing their sacred commission ever since the reign of Josaphat; during which time Eliseus, Micheas, Jehu, and others, lived, chap. xx. 14., and xviii. 27. (Calmet)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Priest, refers to Zacharias. Septuagint, “Azarias.” (Haydock) — It is not certain whether this was the person whom Jesus Christ speaks, (Matthew xxiii. 35.; Calmet) as St. Jerome believes; (Matthew xxiii. 35.) or our Saviour alludes to one of the minor prophets, (Tirinus) or to the father of John the Baptist. [Luke i.] (Baronius) (Haydock) — Sight. Hebrew, “above,” perhaps on the steps between the two courts. (Calmet) — To make. Hebrew, “therefore hath he forsaken you.”"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Require it.. I commit my cause into his hands. (Menochius) — The vengeance of the Lord was not slack. (Haydock)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Damascus. Hazael had before invaded Jerusalem, 4 Kings xii. 17. The Syrian army, which routed Juda, was comparatively small. (Calmet) — The idolatrous princes now received the due punishment of their crimes, ver. 17."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Shameful, is not expressed in Hebrew. But the putting a king to the torture, (ver. 25.) and upbraiding him with his ingratitude and perfidy, was a great indignity. (Haydock) — He had probably entered into engagements with Hazael, when he prevailed on him to depart, the year before. (Calmet) — Nabuchodonosor treated Sedecias in like manner, 4 Kings xxv. 6. The author of the Hebrew Trad. among the works of St. Jerome, (Haydock) asserts that the children of Joas were slain before his eyes, while the Syrians reproached him for the unjust murder of Zacharias. (Calmet)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Servants. By whom the man is slain, who had murdered his spiritual father. (Worthington) — Son. Hebrew, “sons.” See ver. 7. Some think that the brothers of Zacharias were slain, that they might not attempt to revenge his death. — Kings. These sepulchres are amazingly beautiful and spacious. The doors are six feet high, and formed, like the rest, of solid stone, cut in the most elegant manner. (Doubdan 26.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Moabitess. Septuagint read, “the Ammonite….and Moabite.” See 4 Kings xii. 21. (Haydock)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","And. Septuagint, “And all his sons: for five came upon him,” with the rest of the conspirators. (Haydock) — They have read differently. (Calmet) — Sum. Hebrew, “the greatness of the burden on him,” (Haydock) imposed by the Syrians, (ver. 24) or denounced by the prophets. Mossa is often taken for a prophetic menace. (Calmet) — More. Hebrew, “in the researches.” Protestants, “story of the book.” Addo entitled his book Modross, chap. xxii. 22. (Haydock) — Only some extracts from these works have come down to us. Esdras had them before him. The abridgments have occasioned their loss, as has been the case with many other excellent works. This was the more easy, as writing was not then so common. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[17,25,0,"The Twelve Books on the Institutes of the Coenobia","Book 12",0,435,"… \"But after the death of Jehoiada the princes went in and worshipped the king: and he was soothed by their services and hearkened unto them. And they forsook the temple of the Lord, the God of their fathers, and served groves and idols, and great wrath came upon Judah and Jerusalem because of this sin. And after a little: \"When a year was come about, the army of Syria came up against him: and they came to Judah and Jerusalem, and killed all the princes of the people, and they sent all the spoils to the king to Damascus. …"],[20,21,1,"Letters","Epistle 54",1,258,"… The priest of God holding fast the Gospel and keeping Christ's precepts may be slain; he cannot be conquered. Zacharias, God's priest, suggests and furnishes to us examples of courage and faith, who, when he could not be terrified with threats and stoning, was slain in the temple of God, at the same time crying out and saying, what we also cry out and say against the heretics, \"Thus saith the Lord, Ye have forsaken the ways of the Lord, and the Lord will forsake you.\""]],"fau":[["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["cyprian","Cyprian of Carthage",0,"father",null,0,258]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 5 (1886)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf05"]]}